WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.250
Speculative Evolution or Speculative Biology
can be defined as a genre focused on hypothetical

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:16.650
scenarios in the evolution of life. Today,
Speculative Evolution is everywhere — from

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:22.500
video games, to graphic novels, to any of
the hundreds of amazing online projects that

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.570
explore worlds beyond our own. But… how
did we get here? Interest in the biology of

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:33.840
fictional life wasn’t always so widespread.
Monsters from ancient myth weren’t typically

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.100
of thought to be part of an ecosystem, they
just kind of… existed. The origin and rise

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.440
of Speculative Evolution as we know it today
is a fascinating tale — deeply intertwined

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.960
with scientific advancement, and full of unexpected
twists. So, for this special entry into the

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.940
archive, I’ll give you the full tour of
this genre’s incredible history, and reveal

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:05.939
the ‘evolution’ of Speculative Evolution…
Let’s start by winding back the clock to…

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:12.030
1895. It’s been almost forty years since
Charles Darwin published his theory of evolution

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:17.079
— this foundational idea that all species
gradually change over time, and through the

00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:21.979
process of natural selection become better
suited to their environment. And by the turn

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:27.240
of the century, the scientific community and
the majority of the educated public had accepted

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:34.350
evolution as a fact. At this time, in a small
town in the English countryside, science-teacher-turned-author

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:40.090
H. G. Wells came up with an idea for a story.
Called ‘The Time Machine,’ the novel follows

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:45.820
an intrepid English inventor on his journey
into an imagined future. The novel represented

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:51.460
a lot of firsts for science fiction, and has
been adapted into numerous films, most famously

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:57.880
in 1960. In both the novel and the film, upon
arriving into the distant future using his

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:03.149
titular ‘time machine,’ the protagonist
realizes that Darwinian evolution has changed

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:08.550
the English countryside beyond recognition.
Not only is the world now populated with strange

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:14.510
plant life unfamiliar to the time traveler,
but humans have evolved as well: having split

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:21.560
into a prey species — the uncanny, childlike
Eloi, and a predator species — the secretive,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:28.239
subterranean Morlocks. The 1960 film portrays
the Morlocks as these weird, yeti-like things

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:33.129
— an effect that hasn’t aged particularly
well. But their description in the novel is

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:38.880
more ominous, with the narrator likening their
appearance to subterranean fish, their bodies

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.680
slick and bleached from millennia spent adapting
to the caverns. Most of the ‘science’

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:50.019
in this section is now somewhat outdated,
but the story doesn’t end here. While this

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:55.750
first section of the novel is the most popular,
and the only one covered in film adaptations,

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:01.290
the truly groundbreaking chapters come after.
The time traveler then journeys millions of

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:08.390
years forwards, and encounters a barren version
of earth lit by a dying red sun. On a ruined

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:14.260
shoreline, the time traveler sees that giant
crab-like creatures have evolved and now keep

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:20.510
a grim vigil over this dying land — a sight
that nearly shatters his psyche. Pushing even

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:26.140
further into the future, he witnesses the
final days of Earth, where upon a blood-red

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:32.260
sea, an indescribable mass of tentacles rises
from the water — a visual so beyond his

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:39.629
19th century comprehension it causes his mind
to collapse. Fitting for a novel about time

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:45.379
travel, this section feels impossibly ahead
of its time. It doesn’t read like something

00:03:45.379 --> 00:03:51.310
written over thirty years before the invention
of sliced bread. By speculating on what life

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:56.719
might look like millions of years beyond his
own time, Wells had inadvertently opened the

00:03:56.719 --> 00:04:02.799
door to a whole new literary genre — the
genre of Speculative Evolution. “The people

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:09.939
of Mars probably walk on their hind legs.
Their barrel-chested bodies covered in a coating

00:04:09.939 --> 00:04:17.380
of down. Their ample skulls would be crammed
with intelligence. And their Trunk-like noses

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:24.340
indispensable for feats of engineering…”
The next great frontier in Speculative Evolution

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:29.530
would emerge that same year, when astronomer
Percival Lowell produced an account of what

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:35.330
he believed to be canals on the surface of
Mars. What we now know to be an optical illusion

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:40.169
caused by streaks of dust was at the time
believed by some to be evidence that there

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:46.490
was complex life on the next rock over from
Earth. Inspired by these findings, H.G. Wells

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.820
would publish ‘The War of the Worlds,’
just two years after ‘The Time Machine’

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:56.870
— a novel notable for popularizing the alien
invasion story. Wells’s tale about Martians

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:01.930
invading Earth is widely interpreted as a
commentary on British Imperialism, yet ‘War

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:07.250
of the Worlds’ could also be considered
another founding work of Speculative Evolution.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.630
Before the book’s release, Wells published
numerous articles speculating about what life

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:18.080
on Mars might look like — discussing his
theories for how plant and animal life might

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:24.449
evolve. We now know Mars can’t support life
for… a range of reasons, but at the time,

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:29.210
this was ‘reasonable’ scientific conjecture.
It’s interesting to read these articles

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:33.680
because they really feel like someone gushing
about a speculative world they’ve created.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.610
And the early ideas for the Martians and their
planet Wells outlines are almost unchanged

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:44.520
in ‘War of the Worlds’ -- which suggests
this little project inspired the massively

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.970
influential follow up. One of the most important
sci fi novels began with Wells dreaming about

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:58.259
speculative ecosystems… “Today a space-conscious
public avidly consumes tons of story material

00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:03.690
about life on other planets.” In the 20th
century, The War of the Worlds would inspire

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:09.629
numerous other invasion stories, which tended
to echo public perceptions of threat. Bolstered

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:16.069
by reports of supposed UFOs and a rising public
interest in everything ‘space’ related,

00:06:16.069 --> 00:06:20.919
science fiction stories based on threats from
the stars became a common form of entertainment.

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:26.460
Yet with a few exceptions, these aliens weren’t
quite ‘speculative’ in the way Wells’s

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:32.069
early work was — they were more things that
went bump in the night. What ecosystems or

00:06:32.069 --> 00:06:37.550
evolutionary path created these aliens was
rarely at the forefront of these stories.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:43.220
They were, essentially, fantasy monsters — with
their speculative biology an afterthought

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:50.150
at best. In the 1950s especially, the aliens
of Hollywood came to represent fears of communist

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:55.620
infiltration — with the paranoia that agents
of the enemy were everywhere manifesting as

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:01.580
literal aliens in disguise. “Through the
years, there have been frequent rumors that

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:07.010
Martians disguised as earthmen walk amongst
us. These stories are perhaps strengthened

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:12.900
by thousands of reports of unidentified flying
objects, passing in an endless procession

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:18.200
across our skies…” While the initial speculative
intrigue that inspired Wells to write ‘The

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.260
War of the Worlds’ in the first place had
largely been forgotten by the mid-point of

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:30.460
the 20th century, some lesser-known projects
did emerge during this time. In 1957, animation

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:36.050
director Ward Kimball got funding from Walt
Disney to create ‘Mars and Beyond,’ an

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:40.370
animated special most famous for depicting
what the conditions on Mars might be like

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:46.830
given sufficient water. With advice from astronomer
Dr. E. C. Slipher, Kimball and his team brought

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:52.030
to life an impressively ‘alien’ array
of speculative lifeforms — like trilobite

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:58.240
creatures that can endure Martian dust storms,
fast-growing carnivorous plant life, and a

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:04.500
slew of predators, including one that incapacitates
prey with poisonous gas, and another that

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:11.300
stuns with ultrasonic waves. Perhaps most
notable of all, one sequence also speculates

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:18.310
on life based on entirely new chemical patterns,
imagining life based on silicon instead of

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:23.800
carbon — providing a range of surreal new
forms. While ‘Mars and Beyond’ would be

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:29.199
largely forgotten over the following decades,
the piece stands as remarkably innovative

00:08:29.199 --> 00:08:36.650
by the standards of its time... Throughout
the same period, running parallel to the wave

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:41.710
of aliens were stories which followed in the
footsteps of ‘The Time Machine’ and explored

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:49.029
future and alternate evolution on Earth. In
1930, author Olaf Stapledon released the novel

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:53.980
‘Last and First Men,’ which chronicles
a speculative future history of humanity across

00:08:53.980 --> 00:09:00.230
two billion years and eighteen human species.
The book is somewhat unwieldy, but it did

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:05.940
anticipate the invention of genetic engineering.
The next major work that didn’t involve

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:12.930
aliens came in 1961, with the account of naturalist
Harald Stümpke’s expedition to an island

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:19.890
chain in the south Pacific. The report describes
in detail a previously unknown order of mammals

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:26.029
known as the Snouters. These mammals are distinguished
by the nasarium — a structure derived from

00:09:26.029 --> 00:09:31.990
their nose — that has been adapted for all
sorts of purposes. Reportedly, some used their

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:38.340
noses for walking, others for ensnaring prey,
and still others for rudimentary flight. If

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.860
all this sounds too fantastical to be real…
that’s because it was a total fabrication.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.880
Harald Stümpke wasn’t a real person, but
the pseudonym of the real author Gerolf Steiner

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.630
— a zoologist who wrote and published his
book on the Snouters to prank his colleagues.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:10:00.070
Yet the Snouters mark several important firsts
in the history of Speculative Evolution, containing

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:05.160
a detailed taxonomic tree and a surprising
amount of thought put into the niches each

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:10.700
Snouter fills. In fact, the tone itself is
so dry and scholarly that it successfully

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:16.410
mimics a scientific work, and has become something
of a favorite prank among zoologists, with

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:21.311
several scientists and publishers having written
about the Snouters since as if they were real

00:10:21.311 --> 00:10:26.700
to keep the hoax alive. And when scientists
actually discovered a new species of rodent

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:33.350
with a uniquely large nose in 2015, they officially
named the animal a ‘Sulawesi Snouter’

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:40.560
in honor of Steiner’s fictious creatures.
While the Snouters project was limited to

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:47.360
a single island archipelago, two decades later,
in 1981, author Dougal Dixon would forever

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:53.660
change the genre with his book ‘After Man.’
Set 50 million years from now, ‘After Man’

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:59.490
explored the biology of speculative lifeforms
that have evolved following a mass extinction.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:04.790
To this day, ‘After Man’ is recognized
as the first truly large-scale speculative

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:11.480
evolution project involving an entire world
with a vast array of species. ‘After Man’

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:16.810
also marks the first time the terms ‘Speculative
Evolution’ and ‘Speculative Biology’

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:22.180
were used in writing to describe a work. Some
of my favorite creatures from this project

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:27.139
include the Rabbucks, which are rabbits that
have evolved to fill the grazing niche of

00:11:27.139 --> 00:11:32.709
deer. Or the leviathan sized Vortex, filter
feeders evolved from penguins that fill the

00:11:32.709 --> 00:11:38.320
role left behind by whales. Perhaps the most
famous creature from “After Man’ is the

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:44.720
Night Stalker: a nightmarish, fully-terrestrial
descendent of the humble bat. Dixon himself

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:49.730
has stated the final chapters of Wells’s
‘The Time Machine’ were his primary influence

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:54.920
in creating ‘After Man,’ and the book
can be considered a rebirth of the genre.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:12:01.170
The volume enjoyed widespread success across
the globe, and was even made into a stop motion

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:06.971
animated adaptation in Japan — although
the project was never translated. Following

00:12:06.971 --> 00:12:11.570
up on ‘After Man’s success, Dixon would
create ‘The New Dinosaurs’ — a book

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:16.760
that explored a hypothetical alternate Earth
where the Cretaceous-Paleogene extinction

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:23.139
event never occurred, leading non avian dinosaurs
to evolve into the present day. ‘The New

00:12:23.139 --> 00:12:28.541
Dinosaurs’ is one of the first entries in
the ‘alternate timeline’ subgenre of speculative

00:12:28.541 --> 00:12:34.490
evolution. Yet things became more complicated
with Dixon’s third book — the bizarre

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:40.350
‘Man After Man,’ which explores the evolution
of future humans. Various higher ups altered

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:45.350
the book heavily during development, and Dixon
himself was reluctant to be involved in the

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:50.540
final version of the project due to its lack
of scientific detail. It did give us this

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:55.420
famous image which has been turned into a
meme, but following the book’s publication,

00:12:55.420 --> 00:13:03.200
no projects like it were released for some
time... In the early 90s, a different kind

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:08.390
of speculative work would arrive on the scene
in the form of ‘Expedition.’ Created by

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:14.290
artist and writer Wayne Barlowe, ‘Expedition’
surveyed the alien ecosystem of the fictional

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:21.310
planet Darwin IV with the same level of detail
that Dixon used when exploring future timelines.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:26.540
The creatures of Darwin IV were designed to
be "truly alien", with Barlowe having grown

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:31.730
dissatisfied with the common science fiction
trope of alien life being similar to life

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:37.360
on Earth. At the time of the book’s publication,
the kinds of aliens you’d see on TV were

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:43.779
mainly actors with things glued to their foreheads.
Yet the organisms of Darwin IV felt authentically

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:49.949
like they’d emerged from a different evolutionary
line, with none of them possessing eyes, hair

00:13:49.949 --> 00:13:56.310
or external ears. And the most intelligent
aliens of Darwin IV — the bizarre, floating

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:01.699
Eosapiens — break the trend of aliens with
human-like intelligence needing to appear

00:14:01.699 --> 00:14:09.220
humanoid. Following the success of ‘Expedition,’
the book was adapted in 2005 into a CGI TV

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:14.990
special for the Discovery Channel titled ‘Alien
Planet.’ Barlowe served as the design consultant

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:21.500
and one of the executive producers of the
adaptation. Throughout the early 2000s, computer

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:28.310
generated television specials became a surprising
haven for speculative works, including Charley

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:33.430
Foley’s ‘Dragons: A Fantasy Made Real’
— which explored the hypothetical evolution

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:39.860
of a lost order of dragons. Joanna Adams’
‘The Future is Wild’ also came out around

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:46.210
this time, which explores future ecosystems
in a nature documentary format. Dougal Dixon

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:51.300
was actually one of the scientists who helped
develop the project, and helped design many

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:56.720
of the lifeforms. ‘The Future is Wild’
is a speculative project that remains unparalleled

00:14:56.720 --> 00:15:01.500
in terms of scale and ambition, and there’s
never really been anything like it produced

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:08.579
since. During the later 2010s, Speculative
Evolution had a significant, if often overlooked

00:15:08.579 --> 00:15:14.050
impact on popular culture. Dixon’s ‘The
New Dinosaurs’ heavily influenced the direction

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:19.290
of Peter Jackson’s ‘King Kong.’ Even
James Cameron’s ‘Avatar’ — which remains

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:24.870
one of if not the most successful film of
all time — owes heavy inspiration to art

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:32.370
Wayne Barlowe created for the movie... At
the same time that speculative evolution was

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:39.480
influencing mainstream media, there was also
an explosion of availability in online projects.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:45.170
And leading the charge for early internet
speculative works was one C. M. Kosemen. Born

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:50.000
in Turkey, the young artist and writer released
the book ‘All Tomorrows’ onto the internet

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:56.730
in 2006. A fascinating and disturbing narrative
exploring future human evolution — both

00:15:56.730 --> 00:16:02.139
through genetic engineering and natural means
— the book is something to behold, at once

00:16:02.139 --> 00:16:08.370
both nihilistic and strangely hopeful. Taking
inspiration from works like ‘Last and First

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:13.680
Men,’ ‘All Tomorrows’ steadily grew
an online following. Around the same time,

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:18.819
Kosemen also released updates for a story
called ‘Snaiad’ — a worldbuilding project

00:16:18.819 --> 00:16:25.120
exploring the biology of aliens on a fictional
exoplanet. Influenced by works like Barlowe’s

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:30.060
‘Expedition,’ the sheer number of invented
creatures and lineages makes ‘Snaiad’

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:36.029
one of the most biologically diverse fictional
worlds. A few years later, Kosemen would release

00:16:36.029 --> 00:16:41.440
‘All Yesterdays’ with artist John Conway
and paleontologist Darren Naish — a book

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:46.760
that speculated on how dinosaurs might have
looked and behaved. This speculation actually

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:51.700
helped inspire projects like ‘Prehistoric
Planet.’ The book also contained a section

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:57.389
titled ‘All Todays,’ which explored how
aliens might reconstruct earth animals based

00:16:57.389 --> 00:17:03.950
on the fossil record with no knowledge of
their biology… Like species filling the

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:09.530
niches of a new environment, Speculative Evolution
would become a bigger and more complicated

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:15.890
genre than ever thanks to online possibilities.
The early works of artist Alex Ries also hit

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:22.310
the internet around this time, showcasing
stunning depictions of alien life. In particular,

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:27.330
Alex Ries would explore the development of
an intelligent hexapod species called the

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:33.520
Birrin — creating one of the most fascinating
and believable alien cultures across fiction.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:38.130
Leading up to the modern day, Speculative
Evolution became more accessible than ever

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:43.720
before — with a growing community of people
inspired by the genre able to connect over

00:17:43.720 --> 00:19:27.780
vast distances.

